**Read in full:** [Improving compressed air system performance — DOE sourcebook](../sources/Compressed-Air-Performance-DOE.lingua#r_314KZNMGSKES8KZPTXKB8V1JD1). Connect it to the earlier separation, compression, refrigeration and delivery balances.

Define the useful output before reporting efficiency. Gas volume needs a reference state; delivered composition, pressure and time matter. Rejected product, purge, standby power, cooling and regeneration belong in the chosen boundary. A lower compressor power can accompany a larger loss of useful output.

For a stated interval, specific energy is total energy inside the boundary divided by accepted output on a defined amount or reference-volume basis. Keep electrical energy, heat, fuel and recovered heat distinguishable before selecting an accounting convention. Co-products from air separation also require an explicit allocation or a comparison that retains all outputs.

**Work:** Reconcile input power, accepted flow and stored inventory over a demand cycle. Compare two systems with equal annual accepted output, then change the purity or delivery pressure. Explain why their earlier specific-energy figures may no longer be comparable.

**Return:** [Industrial gases, vacuum and high-purity supplies — from molecules to useful service](../Industrial-gases-and-vacuum.lingua#r_3GHQJEYMYEN6JDKSF4PPJ403RF).
